A||||7||SCCA FIERO||Questions about bushings and springs.||||1||
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||07-24-2000||12:06 AM||||I am in the middle of installing poly bushings in my 88 Coupe. The rear was simple compared to the front. I am having some trouble and need some help. The bolts that hold the lower control arms to the frame are rusted inside to the sleeve between the bushing and the bolts. I got the nuts off but can't get the bolts to turn. They are in there at such an angle that I can't swing a hammer at them. I tried cutting the bolts but that sleeve is in the way and I have gone through 4 metal blades in my sawz-all. What do you suggest? Get more blades?<P>Now that I have the springs out I thought about cutting them to give me about a 1"-1.5" drop. How many coils do I need to cut to get 1.5" drop in the front and rear? If I do this is it gonna mess up my alignment when I take it in to get it done? They aren't gonna say my car is sitting too low to get measurements are they? And do I need to bend the spring where I cut it so it looks like the end I cut off?<P>One more thing. Do the rear springs need a compressor or can I just remove the top nut?<p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 07-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||07-24-2000||01:28 AM||||I can't speak to the front bolt problem but for god sakes DON'T REMOVE THE REAR STRUT NUT WITHOUT COMPRESSING THE SPRING!<P>To give you an example, I wanted some springs at the PNP. No compressor on hand. I disconnected the knuckle from the lower control arm, stood on top of the car and remove the nut. When it came loose, the spring shot out and broke the window of the car next to me. The strut bounced and went straight by my head and landed on the front of the car. <P>Never again..||||reg||11
Z||000002||StuGood||07-24-2000||02:25 AM||||For what it's worth, I had to change out a lower control arm on a Chevy Monza once.  The bolts corroded/adhered to the bushing inner sleeve, just as you describe, so I had trouble getting the control arm out.<P>In my case, I <B>could</B> hammer on the end of the bolt, but since the sleeve was stuck to a compliant rubber bushing, the rubber absorbed all the shock.  The bolt didn't move relative to the sleeve at all.  I was getting nowhere slowly.<P>I ended up burning the rubber bushings out with a propane torch (car was outside on bare ground).  <B>Not</B> a fast, neat, nor particularly safe operation - keep kids away from the area.  Burning drops of rubber dripped to the ground, it made lots of smoke, took too much time, and it was generally a mess, but it worked.  After the rubber was completely burned out, the inner sleeve + bolt could slide out as a unit.<P>I'd bet <B>somebody</B> on this forum knows a better way!      ||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||07-24-2000||07:12 AM||||Those bolts are a major pain in the hinny when they're rusted solid. No secret way, just use lots of penetrating oil, heat, and hammer on it forever. There was a topic just recently about the same thing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||coyote||07-24-2000||08:55 AM||||when Oreif and i did the suspension on my 87, I cut 1 full coil of the front spring, it eventually settled to a 1" drop, i also cut 3/4's of a coil in the rear, this did nothing for the ride height. I have since picked up a set of the Eibachs and ORIEF will be installing those. You could cut more of the rear springs, but i think an investment in lowerering springs may be  the way to go.<P>Coyote<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||3400Fiero||07-24-2000||09:59 AM||||The lower control arm bolts were frozen in my car as well - I wound up cutting them out with a blow torch - BE CAREFUL!!  I burnt the inner tie rod boot on both sides!  Anyways, I feel your pain!<P>When I cut my springs (also 88 coupe)  I cut 2 coils out of the front and 1 and a half out of the back.  <P>ALWAYS use a spring compressor in the back.  You can get a way without one in the front.<P>GOOD LUCK !!||||reg||9
Z||000006||Oreif||07-24-2000||10:28 AM||||Since you are changing the bushings, You can cut inside the frame rails thru the bushings. This will get the control arms off.<BR>You could use a solid steel bar to place on the bolt and pound that with a hammer. If you do remember to put the nut on so it is flush with the end of the bolt. As soon a Coyote has a free weekend we'll do his car.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||07-24-2000||11:43 PM||||Finally got one front side done. Ended up buying more blades and cut it out. These bushings were a pain. I got the rear out by heating the sleeve until it melted whatever was holding it to the bushing. I then grabbed it with pliers and pulled it out. The bushing was real easy to pull out after the sleeve was out, but on the front I had to completely burn the entire bushing. What a mess. I still get to do the other side... WOOHOO!!!!||||reg||1
